# Contract: `VaultRepository` (`lib/features/vault/domain/repositories/vault_repository.dart`)

Domain-layer interface owning everything local to the device: PIN storage/verification, biometric
hardware capability, the local biometrics-preference mirror, and the in-memory relock timestamp.
`VaultBloc` and the lifecycle observer depend on this and on nothing in `vault/data/` directly
(RNF06). Registered as a `get_it` `lazySingleton` — one instance for the app's process lifetime,
which is what lets `lastUnlockedAt`/`backgroundedAt` survive navigation without a database
(data-model.md Vault Lock State).

```dart
abstract class VaultRepository {
  // Device PIN
  Future<bool> hasPinConfigured();
  Future<void> savePin(String pin);          // hashes internally (research.md R2); overwrites any prior value
  Future<bool> verifyPin(String pin);         // hashes the input, compares to the stored digest

  // Biometric capability (local_auth never crosses this boundary — see data-model.md)
  Future<BiometricCapability> checkBiometricCapability();
  Future<BiometricAuthOutcome> attemptBiometricAuth();   // success | failed | cancelled | notAvailable

  // Local biometrics-preference mirror (NOT the backend's copy — research.md R6)
  Future<bool> isBiometricPreferenceEnabled();
  Future<void> setBiometricPreferenceLocally(bool enabled);

  // Vault Lock State (in-memory only, data-model.md)
  void recordBackgrounded(DateTime at);
  DateTime? consumeBackgroundedAt();          // reads then clears, so one resume only ever evaluates once
  void markUnlocked(DateTime at);
  DateTime? get lastUnlockedAt;
}
```

- **`hasPinConfigured`/`savePin`/`verifyPin`** → back `DetermineVaultEntryUseCase`,
  `SavePinUseCase`, `VerifyPinUseCase`. None return `Either<Failure, T>` — research.md R5 covers
  why a local hash mismatch or "no PIN yet" is not a backend-taxonomy `Failure`.
- **`checkBiometricCapability`** → called once per `VaultPage` open (not cached across process
  lifetime, since the Edge Cases require detecting an OS-level biometric change *between*
  sessions — e.g. the user disabled Face ID in iOS Settings since the last launch).
- **`attemptBiometricAuth`** → wraps `LocalAuthBiometricDataSource.authenticate(biometricOnly:
  true)` (research.md R1); `cancelled` and `failed` are both routed to the same PIN-fallback UI by
  `VaultBloc` (FR-004 does not distinguish "not recognized" from "user tapped away").
- **`isBiometricPreferenceEnabled`/`setBiometricPreferenceLocally`** → the *local* mirror
  `DetermineVaultEntryUseCase` actually reads; kept separate from `BiometricsPreferenceRepository`
  (which only ever writes to the backend, never reads) per research.md R6.
- **`recordBackgrounded`/`consumeBackgroundedAt`** → `VaultLifecycleObserver` calls these directly
  on pause/resume; `consumeBackgroundedAt` clears the stored value so a second rapid resume isn't
  double-counted against a background period that's already been evaluated.
- **`markUnlocked`/`lastUnlockedAt`** → `lastUnlockedAt` exists for observability/tests; the actual
  relock decision only needs `backgroundedAt` vs. "now" (data-model.md's
  `EvaluateBackgroundResumeUseCase`), not a comparison against `lastUnlockedAt` — the background
  gap itself is what's timed, not time-since-unlock, so a user who re-locks, re-unlocks, then
  backgrounds again immediately starts a fresh gap correctly.

## `DetermineVaultEntryUseCase` (orchestration, domain layer)

```dart
Future<VaultEntryMode> call() async {
  if (!await repo.hasPinConfigured()) return VaultEntryMode.pinSetupRequired;

  final capability = await repo.checkBiometricCapability();
  final preferred = await repo.isBiometricPreferenceEnabled();
  return (capability == BiometricCapability.available && preferred)
      ? VaultEntryMode.unlockBiometricFirst
      : VaultEntryMode.unlockPinOnly;
}
```

This is the only place FR-003's "attempt biometrics first when enabled and available, otherwise go
straight to PIN" rule is implemented — `VaultBloc` calls it once per `VaultPage` open and never
re-derives the branching itself.

## Who depends on this

`VaultBloc` (both setup and unlock sub-states), `VaultLifecycleObserver` (background/resume
timing), `SaveBiometricsPreferenceUseCase` (writes the local mirror before firing the backend
call). No `presentation/` widget calls `VaultRepository` directly — always through a usecase or
through `VaultBloc`.

# Contract: `BiometricsPreferenceRepository`
(`lib/features/vault/domain/repositories/biometrics_preference_repository.dart`)

Domain-layer interface for the one network call this unit makes. Deliberately narrow — a single
method for a single field — rather than a general-purpose account-settings repository; see "Why
this stays narrow" below.

```dart
abstract class BiometricsPreferenceRepository {
  Future<Either<Failure, void>> save(bool enabled);   // PATCH account-settings-update, body: {biometricEnabled: enabled}
}
```

- **`save`** → calls `ApiClient.call('account-settings-update', method: 'PATCH', body: {'biometricEnabled': enabled}, authMode: AuthMode.userSession)`, reusing F000's `ApiClient`/`ErrorMapper` unchanged. The OpenAPI schema for `account-settings-update` accepts `displayName`, `persona`, and `biometricEnabled` as independently-optional fields (`additionalProperties: false`, none individually `required`) — sending only `biometricEnabled` is a valid partial update, not a workaround.
- Return type is `Either<Failure, void>`, not `Either<Failure, Account>`: `SaveBiometricsPreferenceUseCase` (research.md R6) discards the result either way, so there is no reason to decode and carry the full updated-account payload the endpoint returns.
- **Caller discipline** (research.md R6, data-model.md Biometrics Preference): the caller always writes the local mirror *first*, then calls `save()` and ignores both branches of its `Either`. This repository itself does not know that — it is a plain, honestly-typed network call; the "don't block on failure" behavior lives one layer up, in the usecase, where it is independently testable without a fake clock or fake PIN state.

## Data layer

```dart
class BiometricsPreferenceRemoteDataSource {
  BiometricsPreferenceRemoteDataSource(this._apiClient);
  final ApiClient _apiClient;

  Future<Either<Failure, Map<String, dynamic>>> update({required bool biometricEnabled}) =>
      _apiClient.call(
        'account-settings-update',
        method: 'PATCH',
        body: {'biometricEnabled': biometricEnabled},
      );
}

class BiometricsPreferenceRepositoryImpl implements BiometricsPreferenceRepository {
  BiometricsPreferenceRepositoryImpl(this._remote);
  final BiometricsPreferenceRemoteDataSource _remote;

  @override
  Future<Either<Failure, void>> save(bool enabled) async =>
      (await _remote.update(biometricEnabled: enabled)).map((_) {});
}
```

## Test scenarios (contributes toward RNF09's 15/15, data-model.md Backend Failure)

Covered with `mocktail` against `BiometricsPreferenceRepositoryImpl` (mocking `ApiClient`, matching
F000's `auth_repository_impl_test.dart` pattern exactly):

1. `ApiClient.call` returns `Right({...})` → `save()` returns `Right(unit)`.
2. `ApiClient.call` returns `Left(NetworkTimeoutFailure(...))` → `save()` returns the same `Left` unchanged.
3. `ApiClient.call` returns `Left(NetworkOfflineFailure(...))` → `save()` returns the same `Left` unchanged.
4. `ApiClient.call` returns `Left(ValidationFailure(...))` → `save()` returns the same `Left` unchanged (malformed-response case).

## Why this stays narrow

FR-016 bars this unit from building any part of the Settings screen; spec.md's Assumptions say a
"dedicated toggle to change the biometrics preference later... belongs to the Settings screen built
by the RF09 unit." A general `AccountRepository.updateSettings({displayName, persona,
biometricEnabled})` would anticipate that unit's needs (`displayName`/`persona` editing) without
being asked to — the opposite problem from F000's `Failure` hierarchy, where going wide *was*
correct because the error taxonomy is closed and fully known today (F000 research.md R6). Here,
RF09's actual settings-screen shape isn't known yet, so the narrow interface is the version that
can be reused or widened later without this unit having guessed wrong.

# Contract: Background-resume relock (`VaultLifecycleObserver` + `EvaluateBackgroundResumeUseCase`)

Covers FR-007–FR-010 and User Story 1's background-resume scenarios, plus the Edge Cases'
"process killed while backgrounded" and "exactly at the 5-minute boundary" cases. This is the
mechanism that is independent of which screen (`VaultPage` included) happens to be on top when the
app backgrounds.

## `EvaluateBackgroundResumeUseCase` (domain layer, pure)

```dart
class EvaluateBackgroundResumeUseCase {
  const EvaluateBackgroundResumeUseCase();

  static const backgroundTimeout = Duration(minutes: 5);   // FR-009 — the ONLY place this constant is defined

  bool call({required DateTime? backgroundedAt, required DateTime resumedAt}) {
    if (backgroundedAt == null) return false;
    return !resumedAt.difference(backgroundedAt).isNegative &&
        resumedAt.difference(backgroundedAt) >= backgroundTimeout;
  }
}
```

- No I/O, no `package:flutter` import, no settings read — testable with plain `DateTime` literals,
  including the exact-boundary case the Edge Cases call out ("fewer than 5 minutes elapsed never
  re-prompts; 5 minutes or more always does") as a direct unit test:
  `call(backgroundedAt: t, resumedAt: t.add(Duration(minutes: 5)))` must be `true`;
  `t.add(Duration(minutes: 4, seconds: 59))` must be `false`.
- `backgroundTimeout` lives here, not as a `VaultRepository` field or a `VaultBloc` constant — one
  definition, referenced by tests and (if ever needed) by presentation, never duplicated.

## `VaultLifecycleObserver` (presentation layer, `WidgetsBindingObserver`)

```dart
class VaultLifecycleObserver extends StatefulWidget {
  const VaultLifecycleObserver({required this.child, super.key});
  final Widget child;
  // ...
}

class _VaultLifecycleObserverState extends State<VaultLifecycleObserver>
    with WidgetsBindingObserver {
  @override
  void didChangeAppLifecycleState(AppLifecycleState state) {
    final repo = getIt<VaultRepository>();
    switch (state) {
      case AppLifecycleState.paused:
        repo.recordBackgrounded(DateTime.now());
      case AppLifecycleState.resumed:
        final backgroundedAt = repo.consumeBackgroundedAt();
        final shouldRelock = const EvaluateBackgroundResumeUseCase()
            .call(backgroundedAt: backgroundedAt, resumedAt: DateTime.now());
        if (shouldRelock && repo.lastUnlockedAt != null) {
          rootNavigatorKey.currentState!.push(
            MaterialPageRoute<void>(
              builder: (_) => BlocProvider<VaultBloc>(
                create: (_) => getIt<VaultBloc>(),
                child: const VaultPage(mode: VaultMode.relock),
              ),
            ),
          );
        }
      default:
        break;
    }
  }
}
```

- **`repo.lastUnlockedAt != null` guard** — a relock push only ever makes sense once the vault has
  actually been passed at least once this process. Without this guard, backgrounding the app while
  still sitting on `LoginSignupPage` (unauthenticated, no session, nothing to protect yet) could
  spuriously push a vault screen with nothing underneath worth protecting. This mirrors
  `SessionBloc._onAuthExpiredDetected`'s own re-entrancy guard
  (`if (state is SessionStatusExpiredRenewable) return;`) — both exist to stop a cross-cutting
  observer from firing when its precondition screen isn't actually the one in front.
- Installed exactly once, in `main.dart`, wrapping `MaterialApp.router`'s `builder` child — the
  same spot the existing session-expired `BlocListener` already wraps (`main.dart`, current
  `CerneApp.build`). Both can coexist in the same `builder` without interacting: a `VaultPage`
  relock push and a `showSessionExpiredOverlay` dialog push are independent root-navigator
  pushes, and neither depends on the other's state.
- Not testable with `flutter_test`'s widget tests in the way `bloc_test` tests
  `EvaluateBackgroundResumeUseCase` — `WidgetsBindingObserver` lifecycle callbacks can be
  simulated via `tester.binding.handleAppLifecycleStateChanged(...)` in a widget test; that test
  belongs to `tasks.md`, not this plan.

## Who depends on this

Nothing in `domain/` or `data/` depends on `VaultLifecycleObserver` — it is purely a
presentation-layer wiring point that calls two already-contracted `VaultRepository` methods and
one pure usecase. `main.dart` is the only call site.

